ough any other point of \\(U_n\\).  \n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell_i\\) can meet each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point, and we have already ensured that the only intersection with \\(U_n\\) is the chosen point \\((a_i,b_i)\\).\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny, distinct, and together with \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 4 (reaching exactly \\(n\\) lines).*  \n   The total number of lines constructed so far is \\(k+3\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(k+3\\le n\\).  \n   If \\(k+3=n\\) we are done.  \n   If \\(k+3<n\\), add \\(n-(k+3)\\) further sunny lines, each passing through any already covered point (for instance through \\((1,1)\\)) with a new slope that is distinct from all previously used slopes and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Adding such lines cannot destroy the covering property, because they only increase the set of lines that contain already covered points.\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)), the configuration would have at most one (respectively zero) non‑sunny line, contradicting Lemma 2, which asserts that at least two lines must be non‑sunny.  \n   Hence such values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary condition (4) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad \n|D_c|=c-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_c\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, **at least two** of the lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line is non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** The non‑sunny line is vertical, i.e. \\(x=1\\).  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(1\\) are covered.  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n-1\\) points.  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\) (otherwise it would be vertical).  \nHence covering \\(C\\) already requires \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nTogether with the vertical line we have used \\(n\\) lines, leaving **no** line to cover any point whose first coordinate is \\(\\ge2\\).  \nBut such points exist (for example \\((2,2)\\)), a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** The non‑sunny line is horizontal, i.e. \\(y=1\\).  \nThe argument is symmetric to Case 1, using the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Case 3.** The non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\); thus it coincides with some diagonal \\(D_{c_0}\\) (\\(2\\le c_0\\le n+1\\)).  \nNow look at the first column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nand the first row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets contain \\(n\\) points.  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\) (otherwise it would be vertical) and at most one point of \\(R\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \nConsequently, covering all points of \\(C\\cup R\\) would need at least \\(n+n=2n\\) lines, which is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nThus every possible placement of at most one non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction; therefore **at least two** lines must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 3  \nIf \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Directly from Lemma 2. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Three fixed non‑sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\; y=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_v:\\; x=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_d:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Points still uncovered after step 1**  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   U_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(c\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the diagonal \\(D_c\\) contributes the points of \\(U_n\\) with \\(a+b=c\\); there are \\(c-3\\) such points.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |U_n|=\\sum_{c=4}^{n}(c-3)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering \\(U_n\\)**  \n\n   Choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in U_n\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i=\\Bigl\\{0,\\infty,-1\\Bigr\\}\\cup\n        \\Bigl\\{\\frac{b_j-b_i}{a_j-a_i}\\;\\bigm|\\;j\\neq i\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(S_i\\) is a finite set of slopes.  Pick a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\notin S_i\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-b_i=\\alpha_i\\,(x-a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties.*  \n   - \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   - \\(\\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\); the lines are distinct.  \n   - For any \\((a',b')\\in U_n\\setminus\\{(a_i,b_i)\\}\\) we have \\(\\frac{b'-b_i}{a'-a_i}\\neq\\alpha_i\\); consequently \\(\\ell_i\\) does **not** pass through any other point of \\(U_n\\).  \n   - By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell_i\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point, so it cannot accidentally contain a point of \\(U_n\\) lying on a different diagonal.\n\n   Therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) covers exactly the chosen point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and no other point of \\(U_n\\).\n\n4. **Counting the lines**  \n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(k+3\\) distinct lines.  Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(k+3\\le n\\).\n\n   If \\(k+3=n\\) we are finished.  \n   If \\(k+3<n\\), add \\(n-(k+3)\\) further sunny lines, each passing through any already covered point (for instance through \\((1,1)\\)) with a new slope that is different from all previously used slopes and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Adding such lines cannot destroy the covering property, because they only increase the set of lines that contain points already covered.\n\nThus we have produced, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nIf \\(k=n-1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)), the configuration would contain at most one (respectively zero) non‑sunny line, contradicting Lemma 2, which forces at least two non‑sunny lines.  Hence such values of \\(k\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Answer  \n\nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
