=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_v\\) or \\(\\ell_h\\);\n   * points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_d\\);\n   * any remaining point belongs to \\(U_n\\) and therefore is of the form \\((2,b)\\) for some \\(b\\) and lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_b\\).\n\n   Thus we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Obtaining fewer sunny lines.*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we simply omit \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines \\(\\ell_b\\) and replace each omitted line by an **additional** non‑sunny line (either \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\) – repetitions are allowed because the problem only requires the lines to be distinct; we can instead add a new vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\neq1\\) or a new horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq1\\) or a new diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq n+1\\)).  \n   Adding a non‑sunny line cannot destroy the covering property, and the total number of sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k\\).  \n   Hence for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)), the configuration would contain at most one (respectively zero) non‑sunny line, contradicting Lemma 2, which forces at least two non‑sunny lines.  \n   Therefore such values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) in that range yields the complete description of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad \n|D_c|=c-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of the same diagonal, the slope of the segment joining them would be \\(-1\\); this contradicts the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), at least two of the lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line is non‑sunny.\n\n*Case 1 – the non‑sunny line is vertical.*  \nWrite it as \\(x=p\\) with some integer \\(p\\).  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(p\\) are covered.  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(p,b)\\mid b=2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n-1\\) points (the point \\((p,1)\\) is already on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) if that line is present).  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical, hence it can contain **at most one** point of \\(C\\).  \nThus covering \\(C\\) already requires \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, leaving at most one line for all remaining points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining points are not collinear (for instance \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are not on a common line), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 2 – the non‑sunny line is horizontal.*  \nSymmetric to Case 1, using the first row instead of the first column.\n\n*Case 3 – the non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\).*  \nThen it coincides with some diagonal \\(D_{c_0}\\).  \nConsider the first column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nand the first row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\) (otherwise it would be vertical) and at most one point of \\(R\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \nConsequently, covering the \\(2n\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\) would need at least \\(2n\\) distinct lines, which is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nAll three cases lead to contradictions; therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 3  \nIf \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n1. **Three fixed non‑sunny lines**\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\; y=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_v:\\; x=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_d:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Their union covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that satisfy \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n2. **Interior points still uncovered**\n\n   \\[\n   U_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) the point \\((2,b)\\) belongs to \\(U_n\\) because \\(2+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering the interior points**\n\n   For every \\(b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_b:\\; y-1=(b-1)(x-1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of \\(\\ell_b\\):*  \n\n   * the slope is \\(b-1\\), which is a positive integer; hence it is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) – the line is sunny;  \n   * \\(\\ell_b\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and through the interior point \\((2,b)\\);  \n   * if \\((a',b')\\in U_n\\) with \\((a',b')\\neq(2,b)\\) then the slope of the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b'-1}{a'-1}\\neq b-1\\) (because \\(a'\\ge2\\) and the fraction is reduced), so \\(\\ell_b\\) contains no other point of \\(U_n\\).\n\n   The slopes \\(b-1\\) for different values of \\(b\\) are distinct, therefore the lines \\(\\ell_b\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **Counting**\n\n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_b\\mid b=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(3+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are sunny (the \\(\\ell_b\\)’s).  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n   * points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_v\\);\n   * points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_h\\);\n   * points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_d\\);\n   * any other point belongs to \\(U_n\\) and is of the form \\((2,b)\\) for some \\(b\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).\n\n   Thus we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) keep the three non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\) and retain only the first \\(k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n   The points that were previously covered only by the omitted sunny lines are still covered by the three non‑sunny lines, because those omitted points all lie on the first row, first column or the outer diagonal.  \n   Consequently the resulting family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nIf \\(k=n-1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)), the configuration would contain at most one (respectively zero) non‑sunny line, contradicting Lemma 2, which forces at least two non‑sunny lines.  \nTherefore such values of \\(k\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Lemma 2) together with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) shows that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
