            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{h}(c):\\; y=c \\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2pt]\n&\\ell_{v}(1):\\; x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{v}(2):\\; x=2,\\\\[2pt]\n&\\ell_{d}: \\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny (they are horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nWe verify that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of these lines.\n\n*Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).*\n\n* **Case \\(b\\le n-2\\).**  \n  The point lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{h}(b)\\).\n\n* **Case \\(b=n-1\\).**  \n  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le 2\\).  \n  If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(\\ell_{v}(1)\\); if \\(a=2\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_{v}(2)\\).\n\n* **Case \\(b=n\\).**  \n  Then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \n  The point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(1+n=n+1\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_{d}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, and none of the lines is sunny. Hence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*The slope of \\(L_{t}\\) is \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence every \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.*\n\nWe now prove that the family \\(\\{L_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).\n\nWe distinguish two sub‑cases.\n\n#### 3.1  Sub‑case \\(b\\le a\\)\n\nSet \\(t=b\\) (note that \\(1\\le t\\le n\\)).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) with this \\(t\\) gives\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{b+1}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(b+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(b+1)y = ba + 1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\le a\\) we have \\(a = b + (a-b)\\) with \\(a-b\\ge 0\\). Using the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\nba + 1 = b(b + (a-b)) + 1 = b^{2} + b(a-b) + 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\le a\\) we have \\(a-b\\ge 0\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\nb^{2}+b \\le ba .\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(1\\) to both sides yields  \n\n\\[\nb^{2}+b+1 \\le ba+1 .\n\\]\n\nBut from (2) we need equality \\( (b+1)b = ba+1\\). The only way equality can hold is when \\(a=b\\). In that situation (2) becomes \\((b+1)b = b^{2}+b\\), which is true. Hence when \\(a=b\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\).\n\nThus every point of the form \\((m,m)\\) (the “main diagonal” of the triangle) is covered.\n\n#### 3.2  Sub‑case \\(b>a\\)\n\nSet \\(t=a\\) (again \\(1\\le t\\le n\\)).  \nEquation (1) with this \\(t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{a}{a+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=a\\) to obtain  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a+1}=a+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((a,a+1)\\) lies on \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nNow observe that any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(b>a\\) satisfies \\(b\\le a+1\\). Indeed, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b>a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb \\le n+1-a \\le (a+1) \\quad\\text{(because }a\\ge 1\\text{ and }n\\ge a+b-1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(b\\) can only be either \\(a+1\\) or equal to \\(a\\). The case \\(b=a\\) has already been handled in Sub‑case 3.1, while the case \\(b=a+1\\) is covered by the line \\(L_{a}\\) as shown above.\n\nTherefore **every** point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\). Since all these lines are sunny, we have exhibited a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\).\n\n*Take the sunny lines* \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) from the construction of Section 3.  \nThese \\(k\\) lines already cover the whole set \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Add \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines* taken from the family of Section 2; for instance we may use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-2\\) together with the two vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) and, if necessary, the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\). All added lines are distinct from the sunny ones because their slopes are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), whereas the sunny lines have slopes \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).\n\nSince the sunny lines already guarantee that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line, the presence of the extra non‑sunny lines does not affect the covering property. The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nwhich completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
