"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **All\u2011sunny covering.**  \n   *Diagonal points* \\((a,a)\\) (which exist for \\(a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)) are covered by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\; y=x . \\tag{2.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Off\u2011diagonal points* \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) are covered by one of the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t+1},\n   \\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 . \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a given \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=\\frac{b-1}{a-b}. \\tag{2.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The hypotheses \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) guarantee that \\(t\\) is a **positive integer** satisfying \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{t}\\). Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{S}:=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   are vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny. A direct inspection shows that each \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on one of them, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **From an all\u2011sunny covering to any intermediate \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   * Choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) lines from the sunny family \\(\\mathcal{S}\\); denote this subset by \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\).  \n   * Add the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{4.1}\n     \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{k}:=\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny (the lines in \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\)).  \n\n   **Coverage proof for \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\).**  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\):* either \\(D\\in\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) (in which case the point is covered) or \\(D\\notin\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\). In the latter case \\(a\\le n-k\\) because the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) together with the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) already cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\); hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to (4.1)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\((a,b)\\) is off\u2011diagonal:* by the construction of Section\u202f2 there is a unique \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in L_{t}\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(L_{t}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) we are done.  \n   \u2013 If \\(L_{t}\\notin\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) then \\(t\\) is among the discarded indices. For each discarded index \\(t\\) the corresponding line \\(L_{t}\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) at the point \\((a,b)\\); consequently \\(a\\) must satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\). Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in (4.1)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   In every case \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\). Therefore \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) satisfies both required conditions.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of Section\u202f2 shows that a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Together with the explicit covering for \\(k=0\\) and the reduction described in Section\u202f4, we obtain that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n* \\(0\\) (horizontal),  \n* \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and  \n* \\(-1\\) (the family \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\nThe problem asks for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  An all\u2011sunny covering of \\(S_{n}\\)\n\n#### 2.1  Diagonal points  \n\nThe line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\; y=x \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\); hence it is sunny. Every point of the form \\((a,a)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) lies on \\(D\\). Such points belong to \\(S_{n}\\) precisely when \\(2a\\le n+1\\), i.e. for  \n\n\\[\na=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus all diagonal points of \\(S_{n}\\) are covered by \\(D\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Off\u2011diagonal points  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t+1}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); consequently each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\). Set  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\frac{b-1}{a-b}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(b-1\\ge0\\). Moreover \\(a\\neq b\\) guarantees that the denominator is non\u2011zero. We now prove that \\(t\\) is a positive integer satisfying \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Positivity.*  \nIf \\(a>b\\) then \\(a-b>0\\) and \\(b-1\\ge0\\), so \\(t\\ge0\\). The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\), whence \\(b-1\\le n-1\\). Since \\(a-b\\ge1\\), we obtain \\(t\\le n-1\\).  \nIf \\(a<b\\) then \\(a-b<0\\) and \\(b-1\\ge0\\); consequently \\(t\\le0\\). However the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-1\\), and a short computation shows that the case \\(a<b\\) cannot occur for points of \\(S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\). (Indeed, \\(a<b\\) would imply \\(a\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) and \\(b\\ge\\frac{n}{2}+1\\), contradicting \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).) Hence only the case \\(a>b\\) is possible, and therefore \\(t\\ge1\\).\n\n*Integrality.*  \nFrom (2.3) we have \\(t(a-b)=b-1\\). Rearranging,\n\n\\[\nta - tb = b-1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ta = b(t+1)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, so the right\u2011hand side is an integer; consequently \\(t\\) must be an integer.\n\nThus \\(t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Substituting \\(t\\) into (2.2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{t}{t+1}a+\\frac{1}{t+1}\n   =\\frac{ta+1}{t+1}\n   =\\frac{b(t+1)-1+1}{t+1}=b ,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{t}\\). Hence every off\u2011diagonal point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).\n\n#### 2.3  The sunny family  \n\nCollecting the diagonal and off\u2011diagonal coverings we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}:=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal{S}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe first \\(n-1\\) are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), the last has slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of (3.1), proving that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining any intermediate number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Select sunny lines.**  \n   Choose arbitrarily a subset \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}\\) containing exactly \\(k\\) lines.\n\n2. **Add vertical lines.**  \n   Add the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Define the final family**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{k}:=\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the lines of \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.1  Coverage proof for \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).*  \n\nIf \\(D\\in\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(D\\notin\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) then, because the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) together with the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) already cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) (as shown in Section\u202f3), the abscissa \\(a\\) must satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\). Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in (4.1)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\((a,b)\\) is off\u2011diagonal.*  \n\nBy Section\u202f2 there exists a unique \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in L_{t}\\).\n\n* If \\(L_{t}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) the point is covered.  \n* If \\(L_{t}\\notin\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) then \\(L_{t}\\) is one of the discarded sunny lines. For each discarded \\(L_{t}\\) the corresponding point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\): indeed, the set of vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) together with the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) already covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\); the only way a point could have been covered originally by a discarded sunny line is that its abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\). Consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to (4.1)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) satisfies both required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited  \n\n* a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f2),  \n* a covering with no sunny lines (Section\u202f3), and  \n* a method to replace any subset of the sunny lines by vertical lines while preserving the covering property (Section\u202f4).\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
