is the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition of the problem, and let  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nThen at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; denote it by \\(L\\) (if it exists).\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Two distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point, and no three distinct sunny lines pass through the same point of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two sunny lines were parallel they would have the same slope, contradicting distinctness. Hence they intersect in exactly one point. If three distinct sunny lines passed through a common point, two of them would have the same slope (by the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the three admissible slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), which is impossible because sunny lines are defined to avoid those three slopes. \u220e\n\nConsequently, the \\(k\\) sunny lines determine exactly \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) distinct intersection points, each belonging to \\(T_n\\) at most once.\n\nEach sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\): indeed, the projection of \\(T_n\\) onto the direction orthogonal to the line has length \\(<n\\), so a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points. Therefore the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n-\\binom{k}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) is