rtices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny** (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*Two distinct sunny lines intersect in exactly one point, and no three distinct sunny lines pass through the same point of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, so they intersect in a unique point.  \nIf three distinct sunny lines met at a common point of \\(T_n\\), then among the three slopes at least two would be equal (pigeon‑hole principle applied to the three forbidden slopes), contradicting distinctness. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. How many points of \\(T_n\\) can a sunny line contain?\n\nFix a sunny line \\(L\\). Because its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point. Since the integer abscissas occurring in \\(T_n\\) are \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\), the line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). The same bound holds if we look at horizontal lines; thus\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every sunny }L. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Counting covered points\n\nLet the \\(k\\) sunny lines be \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\). By Lemma 1 the \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\) intersection points \\(S_i\\cap S_j\\;(i<j)\\) are all distinct, and each such point belongs to \\(T_n\\) at most once. Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n-\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf a non‑sunny line is present (there can be at most one, because any two non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions and would then be parallel), it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the same argument as for sunny lines). Adding this possible contribution we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le k n-\\binom{k}{2}+n. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nRecall \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\). Substituting into (2.3) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2+n\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n0\\le (k-2)n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nConsider the two extreme possibilities \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):* the right‑hand side equals \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{2}<0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\):* the right‑hand side equals \\(-n<0\\).\n\nBoth contradict the inequality, therefore \\(k\\) cannot be \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. Non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the following \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).\n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n-k. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nNow every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with either \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(a=n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (3.1)–(3.2).  \n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) possibly left uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\le n-k-1,\\qquad b\\ge n-k . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), (3.3) forces \\(b=n-k+a\\). Hence the un