classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of integer lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L_1,L_2\\) be two distinct sunny lines. Then  \n\n* (i) \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are not parallel, hence they intersect in exactly one point;  \n* (ii) that intersection point is a lattice point of the plane, and if it belongs to \\(T_n\\) it is the **only** point of \\(T_n\\) common to the two lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, proving (i).  \nIf two distinct lines intersect, the intersection has rational coordinates because each line is defined by a linear equation with integer coefficients (the slope is a rational number, being the ratio of two integers). Hence the intersection is a lattice point.  \nIf the intersection lies in \\(T_n\\) then, because each line contains at most one point of each vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)), the two lines cannot share any other point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can a single line contain?  \n\n*Sunny line.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be sunny. Since its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the map \\(x\\mapsto (x,\\,y(x))\\) is injective; consequently \\(L\\) meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)) in at most one lattice point. Hence  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny line.*  \nThe same argument works for a horizontal line (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), a vertical line (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or a line of slope \\(-1\\); each meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Thus (2.1) holds for **any** line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Inclusion–exclusion bound  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad\nm=n-k=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non‑sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause there are only three forbidden directions, we may have at most three non‑sunny lines; hence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le m\\le 3. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nDenote the sunny lines by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the non‑sunny ones by \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_m\\).\n\nFrom (2.1) each line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nPairs of distinct sunny lines may intersect in a point of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 1 each such intersection is counted twice in the naïve sum and must be subtracted once.  \nTwo distinct non‑sunny lines are parallel (they belong to the same forbidden direction) and therefore have **no** common point; a sunny line and a non‑sunny line intersect in at most one point of \\(T_n\\), but this point is counted only once in the sum \\(\\sum |L\\cap T_n|\\) because the non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points and the sunny line already accounts for that intersection. Consequently the only over‑counting comes from intersections of two sunny lines.\n\nHence, by the inclusion–exclusion principle,\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le kn+m n-\\#\\{\\,S_i\\cap S_j\\cap T_n\\mid 1