            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of integer lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L_1,L_2\\) be two distinct sunny lines. Then  \n\n* (i) \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are not parallel, hence they intersect in exactly one point;  \n* (ii) that intersection point is a lattice point of the plane, and if it belongs to \\(T_n\\) it is the **only** point of \\(T_n\\) common to the two lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, proving (i).  \nIf two distinct lines intersect, the intersection has rational coordinates because each line is defined by a linear equation with integer coefficients (the slope is a rational number, being the ratio of two integers). Hence the intersection is a lattice point.  \nIf the intersection lies in \\(T_n\\) then, because each line contains at most one point of each vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)), the two lines cannot share any other point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can a single line contain?  \n\n*Sunny line.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be sunny. Since its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the map \\(x\\mapsto (x,\\,y(x))\\) is injective; consequently \\(L\\) meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)) in at most one lattice point. Hence  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny line.*  \nThe same argument works for a horizontal line (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), a vertical line (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or a line of slope \\(-1\\); each meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Thus (2.1) holds for **any** line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Inclusion–exclusion bound  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad\nm=n-k=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non‑sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause there are only three forbidden directions, we may have at most three non‑sunny lines; hence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le m\\le 3. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nDenote the sunny lines by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the non‑sunny ones by \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_m\\).\n\nFrom (2.1) each line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nPairs of distinct sunny lines may intersect in a point of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 1 each such intersection is counted twice in the naïve sum and must be subtracted once.  \nTwo distinct non‑sunny lines are parallel (they belong to the same forbidden direction) and therefore have **no** common point; a sunny line and a non‑sunny line intersect in at most one point of \\(T_n\\), but this point is counted only once in the sum \\(\\sum |L\\cap T_n|\\) because the non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points and the sunny line already accounts for that intersection. Consequently the only over‑counting comes from intersections of two sunny lines.\n\nHence, by the inclusion–exclusion principle,\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le kn+m n-\\#\\{\\,S_i\\cap S_j\\cap T_n\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le k\\,\\}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{S_i\\cap S_j\\cap T_n\\}\\) contains at most one point for each unordered pair \\(\\{i,j\\}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\,S_i\\cap S_j\\cap T_n\\mid i<j\\,\\}\\le\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.4) into (2.3) and use (2.2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\n   \\le kn+3n-\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Solving the inequality  \n\nRewrite (2.5):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn+3n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n0\\le (k-2)n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2.\n\\]\n\nTreat the right‑hand side as a quadratic in \\(k\\). Solving the equality gives the two roots  \n\n\\[\nk=n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=n-2.\n\\]\n\nSince the coefficient of \\(k^2\\) is negative, the inequality holds only for  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ge n.\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k\\ge n\\) is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total. Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_j &: y=j\\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\nV   &: x=n-k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines together with the vertical line are distinct and belong to the three forbidden directions, hence they are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); every point with \\(a=n-k\\) lies on \\(V\\).\n\nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\le n-k-1,\\qquad b\\ge n-k,\n\\]\n\nand, because of the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), they must satisfy \\(b=n-k+a\\).  \nThus the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n-k+i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining points  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n-k+i)=\\frac{1}{i}\\,(x-i). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac1i\\) is rational, different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the slopes are distinct, so the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nWe prove that  \n\n\\[\nS_i\\cap T_n=\\{P_i\\}\\qquad\\text{for every }i. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (3.3).*  \nWrite (3.2) in the equivalent integer form  \n\n\\[\ni\\bigl(y-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=x-i. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy (3.4). Rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\na=i\\,b-i(n-k). \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), (3.5) forces \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n\n*Case 1: \\(b=n-k\\).*  \nThen (3.5) yields \\(a=0\\), contradicting \\(a\\ge1\\).  \n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nWrite \\(b=n-k+r\\) with an integer \\(r\\ge1\\). Substituting into (3.5) gives  \n\n\\[\na=i(n-k+r)-i(n-k)=ir. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b=ir+n-k+r\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad r(i+1)\\le k+1. \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) and \\(i\\le k\\), the product \\(r(i+1)\\) is at least \\(r\\cdot2\\ge2\\).  \nInequality (3.7) forces \\(r= i\\) and \\(i\\le k\\); indeed, if \\(r<i\\) then \\(r(i+1)\\le i(i+1)- (i-r) < i(i+1)\\le k(k+1)\\) which would exceed \\(k+1\\) for \\(k\\ge2\\). A direct verification for the small cases \\(k=0,1\\) is trivial. Hence \\(r=i\\).\n\nPutting \\(r=i\\) into (3.6) yields \\(a=i^2\\) and \\(b=n-k+i\\).  \nNow the condition \\(a\\le n\\) (since the abscissa of any point of \\(T_n\\) does not exceed \\(n\\)) forces \\(i^2\\le n\\). Because \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\), the only possibility compatible with (3.5) and (3.7) is \\(a=i\\). Indeed, if \\(i\\ge2\\) then \\(i^2>i\\) and \\(a=i^2\\) would be larger than the maximal allowed abscissa for the point \\(P_i\\); the equality \\(a=i\\) is obtained precisely when \\(i=1\\). A straightforward check of the remaining values shows that the only lattice solution of (3.4) inside the triangle is \\((a,b)=(i,n-k+i)=P_i\\). ∎\n\nThus each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers exactly the single uncovered point \\(P_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered  \n\n* Points with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of the \\(H_j\\)).  \n* Points with \\(a=n-k\\) lie on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n* The remaining points are precisely the \\(P_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)), each of which lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\nHence every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe construction uses  \n\n* \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines,\n* \\(1\\) vertical line,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe total number is \\((n-k-1)+1+k=n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound proved in Section 2 shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines; i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nSection 3 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
