 \nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+1+i\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for such a point we have \\(b=n-k+1+i>n-k\\) (so it is not on any \\(H_j\\)) and \\(a=i\\le k<n-k+1\\) (so it is not on \\(V\\)).  \nConversely, any point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b>n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k\\) must satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b=n-k+1+a\\); thus it is exactly one of the points \\(Q_i\\).\n\nHence the set of uncovered points after the non‑sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{Q_1,\\dots ,Q_k\\},\\qquad |U_k|=k. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining points  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; n\\bigl(y-(n-k+1+i)\\bigr)=x-i . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac1n\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), nor \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Claim.*  The line \\(S_i\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(T_n\\), namely \\(Q_i\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nEquation (3.3) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nx\\equiv i\\pmod n . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nFor any point of \\(T_n\\) the abscissa \\(x\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently (3.4) forces \\(x=i\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=i\\) back into (3.3) yields \\(y=n-k+1+i\\).  \nThus the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) satisfying (3.3) is \\((i,n-k+1+i)=Q_i\\). ∎\n\nHence each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers precisely the previously uncovered point \\(Q_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  The final family of lines  \n\nCollect all the lines defined above:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k},V,S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}, &\\text{if }k<n,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k},S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}, &\\text{if }k=n\\;( \\text{so }n-k=0).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThe family contains \\((n-k)\\) horizontal lines, possibly one vertical line, and \\(k\\) sunny lines; altogether \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on one of the horizontal (or vertical) lines, or is one of the points \\(Q_i\\) and therefore lies on the corresponding sunny line \\(S_i\\).  Hence each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the explicit construction for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) shows that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
