-----------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k<n\\)\n\n**Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\n\\]\n\nand, because \\(k<n\\),\n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\); every point with \\(a=n-k+1\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=\\bigl(i,\\;n-k+1+i\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for such a point we have \\(b=n-k+1+i>n-k\\) (so it is not on any \\(H_j\\)) and \\(a=i\\le k<n-k+1\\) (so it is not on \\(V\\)).  \nConversely, any point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b>n-k\\) and \\(a\\le k\\) must satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b=n-k+1+a\\); hence it is exactly one of the points \\(Q_i\\).  \nThus the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{Q_1,\\dots ,Q_k\\},\\qquad |U_k|=k. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n**Sunny lines covering the remaining points.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; n\\bigl(y-(n-k+1+i)\\bigr)=x-i . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac1n\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n*Claim.*  The line \\(S_i\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(T_n\\), namely \\(Q_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Equation (3.3) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nx\\equiv i\\pmod n . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nFor any point of \\(T_n\\) the abscissa \\(x\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently (3.4) forces \\(x=i\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=i\\) back into (3.3) yields \\(y=n-k+1+i\\).  \nThus the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) satisfying (3.3) is \\((i,n-k+1+i)=Q_i\\). \u220e\n\nHence each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers precisely the previously uncovered point \\(Q_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**The final family.**  \nCollect all the lines defined above:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains \\((n-k)+1+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow no non\u2011sunny line may be used.  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{1}{n}\\,x+\\frac{n+1-i}{n}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac1n\\) is sunny, so all \\(L_i\\) are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nMultiplying the equation of \\(L_i\\) by \\(n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn b = a + n+1-i . \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), (3.6) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\na\\equiv i\\pmod n . \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\n\nThus the unique index \\(i\\) with \\(a=i\\) satisfies (3.6), and then (3.5) forces  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a}{n}+\\frac{n+1-a}{n}= \\frac{n+1-a}{n}+ \\frac{a}{n}= \\frac{n+1-a}{n}+ \\frac{a}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{n}=1+\\frac{n-a}{n}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\) is an integer and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), the only possibility is  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence **\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\)**.  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe trivial bound (2.1) together with the explicit constructions for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) shows that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
