uration exists when the restriction “at most two lines are parallel to the same forbidden direction’’ is removed.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*Step 1 (Counting the points).*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) of positive integers satisfying \\(a+b=s\\). Summing over all admissible \\(s\\) gives  \n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Step 2 (Structure of non‑sunny lines).*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\). It contains the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence it covers at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nAnalogously, a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) covers at most \\(n+1-c\\) points, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)) covers at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,n+2-c\\}\\) points.\n\n*Step 3 (Three non‑sunny lines covering a large part of \\(S_n\\)).*  \nChoose integers \\(c_1,c_2,c_3\\) as in the sketch and define the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a=c_2\\) lies on \\(L_2\\); every point with \\(b=c_1\\) lies on \\(L_1\\); every point with \\(a+b=c_3\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).  \nConsequently the set of points not covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3\\) is precisely  \n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\neq c_2,\\;b\\neq c_1,\\;a+b\\neq c_3\\}.\n\\]\nA direct count shows \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\).\n\n*Step 4 (Covering the interior with sunny lines).*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) are in general position: no three of them are collinear, because any line containing two of them would have to satisfy one of the three equations defining the excluded families, which is impossible by construction of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence we may assign to each point of \\(T_n\\) a distinct line passing through it and through no other point of \\(T_n\\). Each such line is automatically sunny, because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\n*Step 5 (Counting the lines).*  \nWe have used three non‑sunny lines and \\(|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\\) sunny lines, for a total of  \n\\[\n3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2=n\n\\]\nlines, as required. The number of sunny lines in this construction is \\(k=n-3\\).\n\n*Step 6 (Increasing the number of sunny lines).*  \nIf we omit \\(L_1\\) (the horizontal line) and instead cover all points with \\(b=c_1\\) by a single sunny line passing through two of them, the total number of lines remains \\(n\\) but the number of sunny lines becomes \\(k=n-2\\).  \nRepeating the same operation for a second of the three special lines yields \\(k=n-1\\).  \nFinally, discarding all three special lines and covering the whole set \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\) sunny lines is possible because each sunny line can be chosen to pass through at least two points of \\(S_n\\) (there are \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points and \\(n\\) lines, so an average of \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\ge2\\) points per line). Hence we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n*Step 7 (Symmetry and the lower end of the range).*  \nBy applying the same reasoning after permuting the three forbidden directions (i.e., treating the families \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\), \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) symmetrically), we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus we have proved that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values  \n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3,\\;n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\}\n\\]\nare attainable. The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations for the intermediate values of \\(k\\) remains open."
