gurations with  \n\n   \\[\n   k=3,\\;2,\\;1,\\;0 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Realising every intermediate value of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   From (2) we have configurations for \\(k=n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\); from (3) we have configurations for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n   Each elementary replacement described in Sections 7 and 8 changes the number of sunny lines by exactly 1 and never destroys the covering property.  \n   Therefore, starting from any of the extreme configurations, a finite sequence of such replacements yields a configuration with any prescribed integer \\(k\\) between the extremes.  \n   Explicitly:\n\n   *If \\(k\\ge n-3\\):* begin with the base configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) (which has \\(k=n-3\\)) and replace the required number of non‑sunny lines by sunny ones as in Section 7.\n\n   *If \\(k\\le 3\\):* begin with the configuration obtained after three replacements of Section 8 (which has \\(k=3\\)) and replace the required number of sunny lines by non‑sunny ones as in Section 8.\n\n   *If \\(4\\le k\\le n-4\\):* first move from the base configuration to the nearest endpoint (either \\(k=3\\) or \\(k=n-3\\)) and then continue with the appropriate replacements; the total number of replacements equals \\(|k-(\\text{chosen endpoint})|\\).\n\n   In every case we end with a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing precisely the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n10. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n    The total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\); a line is either sunny or non‑sunny, so \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n    The constructions above show that every integer in this interval is attainable.  \n    Hence no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a suitable family of }n\\text{ lines, and only these }k\\text{ are possible.}}\n\\]"
