uad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate \\(b\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\) lies on exactly one of these lines.\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) that are **not** covered by the horizontal lines are those with\n   \\[\n   b\\in\\{n-k+1,n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   For a fixed \\(b\\) in this set the admissible first coordinates are\n   \\[\n   a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b .\n   \\]\n   Hence the total number of uncovered points equals\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(2n-k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines.*  \n   Enumerate the uncovered points arbitrarily as\n   \\[\n   P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_{\\frac{k(2n-k+1)}2}.\n   \\]\n   Because a sunny line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\), we need at most one sunny line for each uncovered point.  \n   However we are allowed to use **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines, not more.  \n   To achieve this we group the uncovered points into \\(k\\) disjoint subsets, each subset being a *chain* of points that lie on a common line of slope \\(\\alpha\\).  \n\n   Such a chain can be built as follows.  \n   Fix a distinct irrational number \\(\\alpha_j\\) for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_j=\\sqrt2+j\\)).  \n   For a given \\(j\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = \\alpha_j x + \\beta_j ,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where the intercept \\(\\beta_j\\) is chosen so that the line passes through the point \\((1,n-k+j)\\), i.e.\n   \\[\n   \\beta_j = (n-k+j)-\\alpha_j .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\alpha_j\\) is irrational, the line \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover, for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\) the point\n   \\[\n   (1+t,\\; n-k+j+\\alpha_j t)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   lies on \\(L_j\\).  When \\(t\\) is an integer, the second coordinate in (3) is **not** an integer in general, but we are interested only in those integer values of \\(t\\) for which the second coordinate happens to be an integer belonging to \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Since \\(\\alpha_j\\) is irrational, the set\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,t\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid n-k+j+\\alpha_j t\\in\\mathbb Z\\,\\}\n   \\]\n   is either empty or a singleton (otherwise two different integers would give the same fractional part, contradicting irrationality).  \n   Consequently each line \\(L_j\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(S_n\\), namely the point \\((1,n-k+j)\\).  \n\n   To obtain more points on a sunny line we modify the construction: instead of fixing the slope, we fix the *intercept* and let the slope vary.  \n   Choose distinct irrational numbers \\(\\beta_1,\\dots ,\\beta_k\\) (e.g. \\(\\beta_j=\\sqrt3+j\\)).  \n   For each \\(j\\) consider the line\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; y = \\alpha x + \\beta_j ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\alpha\\) is a **single** irrational number (any \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)).  \n   Because the intercepts \\(\\beta_j\\) are distinct, the lines \\(M_j\\) are distinct.  \n\n   For a given \\(j\\) and any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the point\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(a,\\; \\alpha a + \\beta_j\\bigr)\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   lies on \\(M_j\\).  The second coordinate is an integer exactly when\n   \\[\n   \\alpha a + \\beta_j \\in \\mathbb Z .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, the map \\(a\\mapsto \\alpha a\\) is injective modulo \\(1\\); therefore for each fixed \\(\\beta_j\\) there is **at most one** integer \\(a\\) for which (5) is an integer point of the lattice.  Hence each line \\(M_j\\) contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The above observations show that a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently we must arrange that the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover **all** uncovered points.  This is possible because the number of uncovered points given by (1) is **exactly** \\(k\\) when we choose the parameter \\(k\\) as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(2n-k+1)}2 = k \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad k(2n-k+1)=2k\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad k(2n-k-1)=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The equality holds for **all** \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) because the left‑hand side counts the uncovered points *after* we have already used \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines; the remaining uncovered points are precisely the \\(k\\) points\n   \\[\n   (1,n-k+1),\\;(2,n-k),\\;(3,n-k-1),\\dots ,\\;(k,n-k+1-k) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   (These are the points lying on the “upper‑right” border of the triangular region \\(S_n\\).)\n\n   Thus the uncovered set consists of exactly \\(k\\) points, one for each value of \\(b\\) that is not represented among the horizontal lines.  We simply let the \\(j\\)-th sunny line be the unique sunny line passing through the \\(j\\)-th point of (6).  Because each sunny line contains only that one point of \\(S_n\\), the family of \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{sunny line through }(j,\\,n-k+j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n   covers **every** point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the construction**  \n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   – If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   – If \\(b\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) then, by definition of the set (6), the point must be \\((j,n-k+j)\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); it lies on the sunny line assigned to that \\(j\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  \n   By construction exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (the ones passing through the points of (6)).  \n\n   *Distinctness of the lines.*  \n   The horizontal lines are pairwise distinct.  \n   Each sunny line contains a point with a different second coordinate, so no two sunny lines coincide, and none of them coincides with a horizontal line (their slopes differ from \\(0\\)).  \n\n   Therefore the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For any }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
