ain at most one point of the **first row**  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nand at most one point of the **first column**  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) intersect in the single point \\((1,1)\\); therefore together they contain \\(2n-1\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince a non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\), it can meet \\(R\\cup C\\) in at most two points (e.g. a vertical line meets \\(R\\) once and \\(C\\) once, a diagonal meets each at most once, a horizontal line meets only \\(R\\)).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil = m\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines are necessary. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Realising any admissible number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(m\\) be as in Lemma 1. For every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-m,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines supplied by Lemma 1; they already cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nAdd \\(n-m\\) further lines, each distinct from the previous ones.  \nChoose any \\(k\\) of these added lines to have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (they are sunny); the remaining \\(n-m-k\\) added lines may be taken, for instance, as horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with new constants \\(c\\) not used before.  \n\nSince the added lines are not required to pass through any point of \\(T_n\\), the covering condition remains satisfied, and the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Characterisation of admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\