ve this bound is that **all** lines used for the covering are distinct, and the number of non\u2011sunny lines cannot be smaller than the construction of Lemma\u202f1. Thus the lower bound coincides with the construction.\n\n5. **Range of \\(k\\)** \u2013 from Lemma\u202f2 the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n     =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\)** \u2013 start with the \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines of Lemma\u202f1 (they already cover all points). Add the remaining \\(n-\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) lines arbitrarily, choosing exactly \\(k\\) of them to have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (sunny) and the rest to be, say, horizontal lines with new constants. The covering condition is unchanged, and the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (A covering with \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nWrite \\(n=2t\\) (even) or \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case* (\\(n=2t\\)).  \nConsider the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le t)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le t\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (1).  \n*If \\(a>t\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(t+1)=t\\); consequently either \\(a+b=n+1\\) (so the point lies on (2)) or \\(a+b<n+1\\) (still on (2) because the whole diagonal contains all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and any point with smaller sum is already covered by a vertical line). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines (1)\u2013(2).  \nThe number of lines is \\(t+1=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n\n*Odd case* (\\(n=2t+1\\)).  \nTake the \\(t+1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le t+1)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_{t+2}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is covered, and the total number of lines is \\((t+1)+1=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n\nThus \\(T_n\\) can be covered by \\(\\displaystyle m:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A sharp lower bound)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nso \\(|R\\cup C|=2n-1\\).\n\nConsider any line \\(\\ell\\) (sunny or non\u2011sunny).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is a straight line, it can intersect the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point and the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point. Hence \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most two** points of the set \\(R\\cup C\\).\n\nSince the family of \\(n\\) lines must cover all points of \\(R\\cup C\\), we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|R\\cup C|}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil = m\n\\]\n\nlines in total.  \nBecause we are allowed exactly \\(n\\) lines, the only way to achieve the covering is to use at least \\(m\\) lines, and the construction of Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines are sufficient. Consequently **any** covering of \\(T_n\\) must contain at least \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the possible numbers of sunny lines\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at least \\(m\\); therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-m\n   = n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConversely, let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nStart with the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines given in Lemma\u202f1; they already cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAdd the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines, choosing exactly \\(k\\) of them to have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (these become sunny) and taking the other \\(n-m-k\\) lines to be horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with constants \\(c\\) distinct from those already used.  \nAll added lines are distinct from one another and from the original \\(m\\) lines, and they do not affect the covering of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) is attainable, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe upper bound follows from Lemma\u202f2 and inequality (5).  \nThe lower bound (realisation) follows from the construction described after Lemma\u202f2. \u220e"

