e \\(x\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\);\n* the \\(y\\)-coordinates are the same list;\n* the sums \\(x+y\\) are \\(2,4,\\dots ,2m\\).\n\nConsequently\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(P\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(P\\);\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and can also contain at most one point of \\(P\\).\n\nThus every non\u2011sunny line meets \\(P\\) in at most one point.  \nSince \\(|P|=m\\), covering all points of \\(P\\) requires at least \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBecause \\(P\\subseteq T_n\\), any family of lines that covers the whole set \\(T_n\\) must contain at least \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nThe total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at least  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-m\n   = n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   = \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Existence of a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\ns=n-m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{vert}}=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{horiz}}=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le s\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny; there are exactly \\(m+s=n\\) of them.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(m=\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\),\n\n\\[\na>m\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=s .\