If \\(k=1\\)* the set \\(S_1=\\{(n,1)\\}\\) consists of a single point.  \nChoose any line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nM_1:\\; y = x-(n-1)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\) and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Thus the single line \\(M_1\\) is sunny and together with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines we have the required configuration.\n\n*Assume now \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{\\,k+1-j\\,}\\,x + \\frac{(k+1)j}{\\,k+1-j\\,}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_j\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{j}{k+1-j}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and \\(k+1-j\\ge 1\\),\n\n* the slope is never \\(0\\) (so \\(M_j\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* the slope is never undefined (so \\(M_j\\) is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* the slope is never \\(-1\\) (since \\(-\\dfrac{j}{k+1-j}=-1\\) would imply \\(j=k+1-j\\), i.e. \\(2j=k+1\\), which has no integer solution when \\(k\\) and \\(j\\) are integers with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).  \n\nHence each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(M_j\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(n-k+j,\\;k+1-j),\\qquad \nQ_j=(n-k+j+1,\\;k-j).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to the list (5); indeed \\(P_j\\) is the \\(j\\)-th element of (5) and \\(Q_j\\) is the \\((j+1)\\)-st element (for \\(j=k\\) the point \\(Q_k\\) lies outside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and is irrelevant).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S_k\\) appears either as some \\(P_j\\) (for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\)) or as some \\(Q_j\\) (for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\)), and therefore is contained in at least one of the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\).\n\nThe slopes in (8) are distinct for different values of \\(j\\); thus the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are pairwise distinct.  Moreover none of them is vertical, so they are distinct from the vertical lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The whole family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nCollecting the vertical and sunny lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n}\\}, & k=0,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-1}\\}\\cup\\{M_1\\}, & k=1,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}, & k\\ge 2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn every case \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) lines are sunny (the \\(M_j\\) when \\(k\\ge 1\\)).  \nBy the discussion in \u00a7\u00a72.1\u20132.3 every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nWe have exhibited, for **each** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nTogether with the obvious bound (2) this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
