{j}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(n-k+1+(k-mj),\\; mj\\bigr)\\mid m=1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{j}\\bigr\\rfloor \\bigr\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(j\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) the families (8) partition \\(T_{k}\\): each point \\((n-k+1+r,k-r)\\) has a unique divisor \\(j\\) equal to the greatest common divisor of \\(k-r\\) and \\(k\\), and that \\(j\\) is exactly the index of the line that contains the point.  Hence every element of \\(T_{k}\\) lies on **some** \\(L_{j}\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe slopes \\(-\\dfrac{j}{k+1}\\) are pairwise different, so the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are distinct.  None of them is vertical, therefore they are distinct from the vertical lines \\(V_{i}\\) of (2).\n\n#### 2.4  The whole family\n\nCollect the lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k=0\\) the second set is empty and \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\nFor \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are sunny.  By construction the vertical lines cover all points with abscissa \\(\\le n-k\\), while the sunny lines cover the remaining set \\(T_{k}\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem, and we have shown that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because only \\(n\\) lines are available.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

