\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore the corresponding\nlines will be sunny.\n\nFor each \\(j\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j :\\; y = m_j\\,x + b_j ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the intercept \\(b_j\\) is chosen so that \\(S_j\\) passes through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j =\\bigl(n-k-1+j,\\;k+2-j\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\n\\[\nb_j = (k+2-j) - m_j\\,(n-k-1+j)\n     = (k+2-j) - \\frac{j}{k+1}\\,(n-k-1+j).\n\\]\n\nBecause the points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)\nare distinct as well.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3  Verification of the covering property\n\n*Columns \\(1\\le a\\le n-k-2\\).*  \nThese columns are covered by the vertical lines \\(L^{(v)}_a\\).\n\n*Column \\(a=n-k-1\\).*  \nAll points \\((n-k-1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+3\\) lie on the horizontal line\n\\(L^{(h)}\\) (since \\(y=1\\) covers the point \\((n-k-1,1)\\)) and on the diagonal\n\\(L^{(d)}\\) (since \\(x+y=n\\) gives \\(b=n-(n-k-1)=k+1\\)).  The remaining two points\n\\((n-k-1,2)\\) and \\((n-k-1,k+2)\\) are respectively on \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_k\\) by the\nchoice of the points \\(P_j\\).  Thus the whole column is covered.\n\n*Columns \\(a=n-k,\\dots ,n-1\\).*  \nFor a column \\(a=n-k-1+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) the admissible ordinates satisfy\n\\(1\\le b\\le k+2-r\\).  The point \\((a,b)=(n-k-1+r,\\,k+2-r)\\) is exactly the point\n\\(P_r\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_r\\).  All points with smaller\n\\(b\\) have the same abscissa and a larger ordinate; they lie on the diagonal\n\\(L^{(d)}\\) because \\(x+y=n\\) gives \\(b=n-a = k+1-r\\ge 1\\).  Hence every point of\nthese columns belongs either to \\(L^{(d)}\\) or to the appropriate sunny line\n\\(S_r\\).\n\nConsequently **every point of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).**\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (\\(n-k-2\\) vertical, one horizontal, one diagonal), and  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\