k\\) of the lines must be non‑sunny and pass through distinct points of \\(C\\); the only possibilities are horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) or lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nA line cannot be both vertical and horizontal.  \nThe only type of non‑sunny line that can belong to both families is a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); such a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and can contain at most one point of \\(R\\) **and** at most one point of \\(C\\) (namely the points \\((c-1,1)\\) and \\((1,c-1)\\)).  \nThus at most **one** line can be counted simultaneously for the two families.\n\nTherefore the total number of non‑sunny lines is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)+(n-k)-1 = n-2k+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the whole family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe construct a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that fulfills the two required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines ( \\(n-k\\) of them)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{(v)}_i &: x=i , &&\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-2,\\\\\nL^{(h)} &: y=1 ,\\\\\nL^{(d)} &: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L^{(v)}_i\\) is vertical, \\(L^{(h)}\\) is horizontal and \\(L^{(d)}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently all of them are non‑sunny.  \nThe number of these lines is \\((n-k-2)+1+1=n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines ( \\(k\\) of them)\n\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=\\bigl(n-k-1+j,\\;k+2-j\\bigr)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\nChoose the slope  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{j}{k+1}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nLet \\(S_j\\) be the line with slope \\(m_j\\) passing through \\(P_j\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=m_j x+b_j,\\qquad \nb_j = (k+2-j)-m_j\\bigl(n-k-1+j\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes \\(m_j\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct, and each of them is sunny.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\n*Columns \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k-2\\).*  \nAll points in such a column lie on the vertical line \\(L^{(v)}_i\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=n-k-1\\).*  \nThe points \\((n-k-1,1)\\) and \\((n-k-1,k+1)\\) belong respectively to \\(L^{(h)}\\) and \\(L^{(d)}\\).  \nThe remaining two points \\((n-k-1,2)\\) and \\((n-k-1,k+2)\\) are \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_k\\), hence lie on \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Columns \\(x=n-k-1+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).*  \nFor such a column the admissible ordinates satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le k+2-r\\).  \nThe point \\((n-k-1+r,\\;k+2-r)=P_r\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_r\\).  \nAll points with a smaller ordinate \\(b\\) satisfy \\(b=n-(n-k-1+r)=k+1-r\\) and therefore lie on the diagonal line \\(L^{(d)}\\) (since \\(x+y=n\\)).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (\\(L^{(v)}_i\\), \\(L^{(h)}\\), \\(L^{(d)}\\)), and  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).\n\nHence exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least two non‑sunny lines, which yields the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) (inequality (1)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
