"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation and basic facts.**  \n   *Define*  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line had slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), the equation can be written \\(y=mx+b\\).  \n   For two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) on this line we have  \n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(b_2-b_1\\) are integers with \\(|a_2-a_1|,|b_2-b_1|\\le n\\), the only way to keep the equality for more than \\(n\\) different pairs is to have \\(m\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), contradicting sunniness. Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a sunny line. \u220e  \n\n   *Corollary 2.* If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of incidences \u201cpoint of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line\u2019\u2019 would be at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\), which is smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \n\n   *Corollary 3.* If exactly one line were non\u2011sunny, that line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the same argument as Lemma\u202f1 works for the three forbidden slopes). The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\) points would have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each contributing at most \\(n\\) points, giving at most \\((n-1)n\\) incidences, which is again smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\). Thus \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.\n\n   Consequently any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   *Step\u202f1 (sunny lines).*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(i\\) (hence different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\neq -1\\)).  \n   Direct substitution shows that the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on every \\(S_i\\); moreover for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-k\\) the point \\((a,\\,i a +1-i)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 1,\\qquad i a +1-i\\ge 1,\\qquad a+(i a +1-i)= (i+1)a+1-i\\le n+1\n   \\]\n\n   holds for the chosen range of \\(a\\). Hence each \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the \\(n-k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,\\,i a +1-i)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\subset T_n .\n   \\]\n\n   The sets of points covered by distinct \\(S_i\\) are disjoint because different slopes give different second coordinates for a fixed first coordinate \\(a\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 (non\u2011sunny diagonal lines).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_j:\\; x+y = j+1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are precisely the lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); they are non\u2011sunny.  \n   For each \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(x+y=j+1\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,\\,j+1-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,j\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All these points are **not** on any of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) because on a sunny line we have \\(y=i x+1-i\\), which together with \\(x+y=j+1\\) would give \\((i+1)x=j\\), impossible for integer \\(x\\) when \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\). Consequently the diagonal lines \\(D_j\\) cover exactly the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 (counting lines).*  \n   We have produced  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);  \n   * \\((n-k-1)\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_{\\,n-k-1}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k-1)=n-1.\n   \\]\n\n   To reach the required total of \\(n\\) distinct lines we add one extra non\u2011sunny line, for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; x = n-k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is vertical (hence non\u2011sunny) and does not coincide with any previously used line because all previous lines have distinct slopes or distinct intercepts. This line contains no point of \\(T_n\\) (its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds the maximal possible \\(a\\) in \\(T_n\\)), so it does not affect the covering property.\n\n   Thus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *Sunny part.* For each \\(i\\) and each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) the point \\((a, i a+1-i)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and lies on \\(S_i\\) by construction.  \n   *Diagonal part.* For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\}\\) and each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,j\\}\\) the point \\((a, j+1-a)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and lies on \\(D_j\\).  \n   *Exhaustiveness.* Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\(b\\ge 1\\) and we can write \\(b = i a+1-i\\) with \\(i = \\frac{b-a+1}{a-1}\\). Because \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the integer \\(i\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is on some sunny line \\(S_i\\). If \\(a> n-k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k+1\\), so \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=j+1\\) for some \\(j\\le n-k-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_j\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility of \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) together with the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are exactly }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite.  \nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nBoth differences are integers whose absolute values do not exceed \\(n\\) (because the coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) lie between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)).  \nIf the line contained \\(n+1\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\), we could find two distinct pairs \\((a_1,a_2)\\) and \\((a_3,a_4)\\) with the same difference \\(a_2-a_1=a_4-a_3\\); the corresponding differences of the \\(b\\)-coordinates would then be equal, forcing \\(m\\) to be an integer ratio of two equal integers, i.e. \\(m\\in\\{0,-1\\}\\), contradicting sunniness. Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n**Corollary 2.**  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of incidences \u201cpoint of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line\u2019\u2019 would be at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\). Since  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nthe covering condition cannot be satisfied. Thus \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Corollary 3.**  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the same argument as in Lemma\u202f1).  \nIf exactly one line were non\u2011sunny, it would cover at most \\(n\\) points, leaving at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=(n-1)n/2\n\\]\n\npoints to be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f1 each of those sunny lines can contribute at most \\(n\\) points, for a total of at most \\((n-1)n\\) incidences, which is still smaller than the required number \\((n-1)n/2\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.\n\nConsequently any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(i\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny_i(a)=i a + 1-i\\ge 1,\n\\qquad\na+y_i(a)=a+i a+1-i=(i+1)a+1-i\\le (i+1)(n-k)+1-i\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality holding because \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThus the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{i,a}:=(a,\\;i a+1-i)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(T_n\\). Consequently each sunny line \\(S_i\\) contains the \\(n-k\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\{P_{i,a}\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i\\neq i'\\) then the second coordinates in (2) differ, so the sets of points covered by distinct \\(S_i\\) are disjoint.\n\n#### 2.2 Non\u2011sunny diagonal lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_j:\\; x+y=j+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nAll integer points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying (3) are  \n\n\\[\nQ_{j,a}:=(a,\\;j+1-a),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\le j\\le n-k-1\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(j+1-a\\ge 1\\); moreover  \n\n\\[\na+(j+1-a)=j+1\\le n,\n\\]\n\nso each \\(Q_{j,a}\\) indeed lies in \\(T_n\\).\n\nA point of the form \\(Q_{j,a}\\) cannot belong to any sunny line \\(S_i\\): if it did, we would have simultaneously  \n\n\\[\nj+1-a = i a + 1-i,\n\\]\n\nwhich rearranges to \\((i+1)a=j\\). Since \\(1\\le a\\le j\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n-2\\), the equality cannot hold for integer \\(a\\) and \\(j\\) in the ranges above. Hence the diagonal lines \\(D_j\\) cover precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) not already covered by the sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3 Adding a dummy line\n\nThe lines constructed so far are  \n\n\\[\nk\\text{ sunny lines }S_i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nn-k-1\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines }D_j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-k-1),\n\\]\n\na total of \\(k+(n-k-1)=n-1\\) distinct lines.  \nTo obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we introduce one further non\u2011sunny line that does **not** intersect \\(T_n\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; x = n-k+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince the maximal possible \\(x\\)-coordinate of a point in \\(T_n\\) is \\(n\\!-\\!1\\), the line \\(L\\) contains no point of \\(T_n\\) and therefore does not interfere with the covering property. It is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and it is distinct from all previously listed lines because none of them is vertical.\n\nNow we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.4 Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\):*  \nSet \\(i = \\dfrac{b-a+1}{a-1}\\) (with the convention that when \\(a=1\\) we take \\(i=b\\)).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\), a short calculation shows that \\(i\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le k\\). Then \\(b = i a + 1-i\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\):*  \nThen \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k+1\\). Write \\(j = a+b-1\\); we have \\(1\\le j\\le n-k-1\\). Consequently \\(a+b=j+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal line \\(D_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nFrom Corollaries\u202f2 and\u202f3 we know that \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are impossible.  \nThe construction of Section\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the complete answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"

