\\]\n   They cover exactly the \\(2k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\}\\cup\\{(a,n-k+1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k\\) sunny lines* (all of slope \\(1\\))  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) and each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\) the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,a}:=(a,\\;a+i-1)\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   belongs to \\(T_n\\) (the inequality \\(a+(a+i-1)\\le n+1\\) follows from \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\(i\\le k\\)).  \n   Thus \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the \\(n-k\\) points \\(\\{P_{i,a}\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n   The three families of points in (4) and (6) are pairwise disjoint, so every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Number of lines.*  \n   We have \\(k\\) sunny lines and the two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k+2\\) lines.  \n   - If \\(k=n-2\\) then \\(k+2=n\\) and we are done.  \n   - If \\(k\\le n-3\\) we add a further vertical line \\(x=n+1\\), which does not meet \\(T_n\\). This yields exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, still with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
