lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nFrom the previous paragraph the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can cover arbitrarily many points of \\(S\\).  \nNevertheless, the total number of points covered cannot exceed \\(|S|\\), so we must have  \n\n\\[\n2k\\;+\\;(n-k)\\cdot 0\\;\\ge\\;|S| \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the inequality \\(2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) would force \\(k\\ge n-1\\), which is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines.  \nA sharper argument is obtained by noting that the non‑sunny lines can each be taken to be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); any such line can be used at most once (the lines must be distinct).  \nThus at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non‑sunny **if** we tried to make all the others sunny, but three lines cannot cover \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\) points for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nConsequently at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  The sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct unordered pairs of points of \\(S\\) as follows.\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S\\) because \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\) and \\((i+1)+(n-i)=n+1\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_i\\) is **not** sunny; to obtain sunny lines we perturb each pair slightly.\nReplace \\(Q_i\\) by \\(Q_i'=(i+1,\\,n-i-1)\\) (still in \\(S\\) because \\((i+1)+(n-i-1)=n\\)).  \nNow the line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i'\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i'=\\frac{(n-i-1)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines are \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\).  \nEach \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i'\\); no other point of \\(S\\) lies on any \\(\\sigma_i\\), and the sets \\(\\{P_i,Q_i'\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint because the first coordinates are distinct.\n\nThus the sunny lines cover precisely the \\(2k\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i'\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  The remaining points\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i'\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered.  \nA direct count shows \\(|T|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k\\).\n\nWe now cover \\(T\\) with \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nTake the following collection:\n\n* **Vertical lines** \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x_{v}\\) where \\(x_{v}=n-k-2\\) (if this number is non‑negative);\n* **Horizontal lines** \\(y=1, y=2\\) (always two of them);\n* **Lines of slope \\(-1\\)** given by \\(x+y=c\\) for the remaining values of \\(c\\).\n\nBecause we have at most three families of non‑sunny directions, we can always choose exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines among them; the precise distribution is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if } n-k\\ge 3:&\\text{use }1\\text{ vertical},\\;1\\text{ horizontal},\\; (n-k-2)\\text{ lines }x+y=c;\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{if } n-k=2:&\\text{use }1\\text{ vertical and }1\\text{ horizontal};\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{if } n-k=1:&\\text{use }1\\text{ vertical (or horizontal)}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nOne checks that every point of \\(T\\) lies on at least one of these lines:\n\n* A point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) with \\(a\\le n-k-2\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a> n-k-2\\) but \\(b\\le 2\\), it is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a> n-k-2\\) and \\(b\\ge 3\\); then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\), so the line \\(x+y=a+b\\) (which is of slope \\(-1\\)) belongs to the chosen family and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nAll the lines are distinct, and none of them is sunny.  \nHence we have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that together cover every point of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\n*Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.*  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
